public abstract class Shape {
    public abstract double getArea();
}

class Circle extends Shape {
    private double radius;

    public Circle(double radius) {
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    @Override
    public double getArea() {
        return Math.PI * (radius * radius);
    }
}

class Rectangle extends Shape {
    private double width;
    private double height;

    public Rectangle(double width, double height) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }

    @Override
    public double getArea() {
        return width * height;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Shape circle = new Circle(5);
        System.out.println("Circle Area: " + circle.getArea());

        Shape rectangle = new Rectangle(4, 6);
        System.out.println("Rectangle Area: " + rectangle.getArea());
    }
}

interface Condition {
    boolean evaluate();
}

class Action implements Action {
    @Override
    public void execute() {}

    public String format() {
        return "";
    }

    public String toString() {
        return "";
    }
}
```

In this program:

1.  We have a `Shape` abstract class that provides methods for calculating the area of different shapes (circle and rectangle).
2.  The `Circle` class extends `Shape` and implements the `getArea()` method to calculate the area using the formula `πr^2`.
3.  The `Rectangle` class extends `Shape` and also implements the `getArea()` method.
4.  We define an `Action` interface that has a method `execute()`. This is just for demonstration purposes; in a real-world scenario, this method would be implemented differently.
5.  In the main class, we create instances of different shapes (circle and rectangle) and call their methods to calculate and print the areas.

This program demonstrates the use of conditional expressions (if-else statements) with abstract classes and interfaces in Java.
